## 5. Discussion

The population of FRBs described here may have a double peak as well as a precursor: the precursor is from the binned ramp-up in luminosity, followed by the main burst at maximum luminosity, and a post-merger burst due to magnetic field shock. Figure 1 shows examples of the luminosity of the BH battery milliseconds before coalescence. When observed with sufficient time resolution, \\sim 0.5 ms, and signal-to-noise, the notable precursor feature emerges. The post-merger burst can manifest due to the migration of the NS magnetic field to the BH at the time of coalescence, and the subsequent magnetic field shock, similar to a FRB from the "blitzar" model. This post-merger burst would occur at least \\sim 0.5 ms after the main FRB, for a 10~{}M_{\\odot} BH, and depending on the NS's intrinsic parameters, and may be as luminous as the main burst, see Eq. (5). While we consider a non-spinning BH in this

